QB.add(72,[{"n":1,"q":"A 25-year-old male presents with fatigue, tremors, jaundice, and cognitive impairment. On examination, hepatomegaly is noted, and an ophthalmologic evaluation reveals a distinctive finding. Laboratory tests show elevated liver enzymes and increased copper levels. What is the underlying pathological mechanism causing this condition?","o":[{"t":"Reduced renal filtration of copper"},{"t":"Increased degradation of ceruloplasmin"},{"t":"Defective biliary excretion of copper"},{"t":"Impaired hepatic metabolism of ceruloplasmin"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Defective biliary excretion of copper"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wilson's disease is a genetic disorder of copper metabolism due to defective biliary excretion of copper caused by mutations in the ATP7B gene."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This leads to copper buildup, primarily in the liver, causing hepatocellular damage, as seen with elevated liver enzymes, hepatomegaly, and jaundice."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Excess copper also deposits in the brain, resulting in neurological symptoms like tremors and cognitive impairment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis is further supported by elevated copper levels, decreased ceruloplasmin levels and presence of Kayser-Fleischer rings, which are copper deposits found in the cornea."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wilson's disease"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wilson's disease, also known as hepatolenticular degeneration, is a rare autosomal recessive disorder caused by the abnormal accumulation of copper in the liver, brain, eyes, and other organs. This results in elevated free copper levels, along with increased copper levels in the liver and urine, while serum ceruloplasmin levels are decreased."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gene Affected: The disease is caused by mutations in the ATP7B gene on chromosome 13. This gene encodes a copper-transporting ATPase responsible for excreting copper from the liver into bile."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathogenesis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Genetic Defect: Mutation in the ATP7B gene. fl"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Impaired Biliary Excretion of Copper"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Copper Accumulation in Hepatocytes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Oxidative Damage"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Excess copper induces oxidative stress in liver cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Results in cellular damage, promoting inflammation and fibrosis in the liver."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Exceeding Storage Capacity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Liver’s capacity to store copper is surpassed."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Copper is released into the bloodstream."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Systemic Accumulation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Circulating copper accumulates in various tissues:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brain: Particularly the basal ganglia, leading to neurological symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Corneas: Deposits as Kayser-Fleischer rings, causing eye issues."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kidneys: Contributes to renal dysfunction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Joints: Causes arthritic changes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tissue Damage"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Accumulation of copper in tissues leads to:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oxidative Stress"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lipid Peroxidation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Enzyme Inhibition Lab parameters of Wilson’s disease"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Parameter Change Explanation Free Copper nn Increased Unbound copper elevated due to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"impaired binding."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Liver Copper nn Increased Accumulation due to defective"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"biliary excretion."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Urinary Copper nn Increased Overflow from the liver leads to high"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"excretion."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Total Copper nn Decreased Mainly composed of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ceruloplasmin-bound copper."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Serum Ceruloplasmin nn Decreased Defective incorporation of copper"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"into ceruloplasmin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reduced renal filtration of copper (Option A): Not the primary defect. The kidney helps excrete copper only after serum levels rise. It's a secondary effect, not the cause."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increased degradation of ceruloplasmin (Option B): Ceruloplasmin is low in Wilson’s disease, but not due to increased degradation—it's due to impaired incorporation of copper into ceruloplasmin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Impaired hepatic metabolism of ceruloplasmin (Option D): Ceruloplasmin production is affected indirectly , but the primary defect is copper transport , not metabolism of the protein itself."}],"qi":[8274]},{"n":2,"q":"A 9-month-old pediatric patient presents with hypotonia, seizures, and delays in achieving developmental milestones. The child exhibits characteristic facial features, including a prominent forehead, sparse eyebrows, and distinct hair appearance. Laboratory results reveal decreased levels of copper and ceruloplasmin. What is the pattern of inheritance for this condition?","o":[{"t":"Autosomal recessive"},{"t":"Mitochondrial inheritance"},{"t":"X-linked recessive"},{"t":"Autosomal dominant"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) X-linked recessive."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient exhibits hypotonia, seizures, and developmental delays, along with characteristic facial features such as a prominent forehead, sparse eyebrows, and kinky hair."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Laboratory tests reveal low levels of copper and ceruloplasmin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These clinical signs are consistent with Menkes disease, an X-linked recessive disorder caused by mutations in the ATP7A gene , leading to impaired copper transport and significant neurological deficits, predominantly affecting males."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Menkes Kinky Hair Disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Menkes Kinky Hair Disease (also known as Menkes disease or Menkes syndrome) is a rare, X-linked recessive genetic disorder that affects copper levels in the body."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is caused by mutations in the ATP7A gene, which is responsible for regulating the transport of copper in and out of cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Presentation:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neurological Developmental delay , hypotonia (poor muscle tone), Symptoms seizures"},{"k":"img","v":9461},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hair Abnormalities"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Failure to Thrive Poor feeding, vomiting, and lack of weight gain"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Connective Tissue Weak bones (osteopenia) , loose skin and joints due to Issues lack of copper-dependent enzymes involved in collagen"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cross-linking"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Characteristic Facial Sagging cheeks, depressed nasal bridge Features"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Blood Vessel Fragile arteries, leading to complications like subdural Complications hemorrhage."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic testing t o identify mutations in the ATP7A gene (gold standard)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plasma copper and ceruloplasmin levels are typically low."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Microscopic examination of hair shows the characteristic kinky or twisted appearance."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No cure for Menkes disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early treatment with parenteral copper histidinate injections may improve outcomes if started early, though it is not fully curative."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supportive care includes managing seizures, nutritional support, and physical therapy."}],"qi":[9461]},{"n":3,"q":"A 22-year-old male presents with symptoms of fatigue, jaundice, and abdominal pain. He also exhibits neurological manifestations, including tremors, dystonia, and personality changes. Laboratory investigations reveal elevated free serum copper levels, and liver function tests show increased ALT and AST. Based on these findings, which area of the brain is primarily implicated in the neurological symptoms of this condition?","o":[{"t":"Corpus callosum"},{"t":"Basal ganglia"},{"t":"Cerebellum"},{"t":"Hippocampus"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Basal ganglia"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical scenario described is consistent with Wilson's disease, affecting the liver and brain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The neurological symptoms, such as tremors, dystonia, and personality changes, are primarily due to copper deposition in the basal ganglia, involved in the regulation of movement and coordination."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The basal ganglia's dysfunction leads to the movement disorders and psychiatric symptoms observed in affected individuals, while the liver damage results in elevated liver enzymes and jaundice."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Features of Wilson's Disease"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hepatic Symptoms Chronic Hepatitis: Jaundice, hepatomegaly, elevated"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"liver enzymes due to copper accumulation in the liver. Cirrhosis: Portal hypertension, variceal bleeding as a result of long-term copper accumulation and fibrosis. Acute Liver Failure: Coagulopathy, jaundice, encephalopathy."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neurological Symptoms Movement Disorders: Tremors (flapping or wind-beating"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"tremor), dystonia, ataxia, dysarthria due to copper accumulation in the basal ganglia. Parkinsonism: Rigidity, bradykinesia, cogwheel rigidity from copper accumulation in the basal ganglia. Psychiatric: Depression, personality changes, cognitive decline."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ocular Features Kayser-Fleischer Rings: Brown/greenish rings in the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cornea due to copper deposition in Descemet's membrane . Sunflower Cataract: Rare, (copper deposition in the lens)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Renal Features Renal Tubular Acidosis (Fanconi Syndrome): Due to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"copper accumulation in renal tubules."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hematologic Features Hemolytic Anemia: Copper-induced red cell damage."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Skeletal Features Osteopenia, osteoporosis, joint arthropathy"}]},{"n":4,"q":"During an eye camp, an elderly patient presented with a distinct ring around the cornea, as given below. The patient was referred to a higher centre for further evaluation. Several weeks later, the patient returned from the higher centre, reporting a progressive loss of vision. Upon examination, it was noted that a cataract had developed. Based on the initial observation and the current condition. Which of the following types of cataracts is most likely to be observed in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Stellate cataract"},{"t":"Snowflake cataract"},{"t":"Oil droplet cataract"},{"t":"Sunflower cataract"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Sunflower cataract"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The initial observation of a distinct ring around the cornea in an elderly patient is suggestive of a Kayser-Fleischer ring, which is typically associated with Wilson's disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Upon the patient's return, the development of cataracts, particularly in the context of Wilson's disease, is most likely to present as a sunflower cataract."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This type of cataract is characterised by a specific pattern of opacification that resembles the appearance of a sunflower and is associated with copper deposition in the lens. Type of Cataract Associated Conditions"},{"k":"img","v":9483},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Wilson's disease (copper accumulation)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sunflower Cataract (Option D) ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":9489},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Trauma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rosette/ Stellate Cataract ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":9494},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diabetes mellitus, certain metabolic disorders"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Snowflake Cataract (Option B)"},{"k":"img","v":9499},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Galactosemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Oil Droplet Cataract (Option C) ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":9505},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Myotonic dystrophy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Christmas Tree Cataract ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":9510},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Down syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blue Dot / Cerulean Cataract"},{"k":"img","v":9515},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Genetic disorders, intrauterine infections (e.g., rubella)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Congenital Cataract"},{"k":"img","v":9517},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Infrared radiation (IR), encountered during glassblowing."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"... (content truncated)"}],"qi":[8274]},{"n":5,"q":"Which of the following investigation parameters is not generally considered definitive for the evaluation of Wilson's disease?","o":[{"t":"Serum copper levels"},{"t":"MRI of the brain"},{"t":"Liver biopsy"},{"t":"24-hour urine excretion of copper"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Serum copper levels"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In Wilson's disease, the accumulation of copper in organs like the liver and brain leads to its characteristic symptoms. Serum copper levels are often low due to reduced ceruloplasmin (the copper-carrying protein), but this is not a reliable diagnostic marker as it can be affected by other conditions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations of Wilson's disease"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Test Findings"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Biochemical Serum Ceruloplasmin: Decreased (<20 mg/dL) - Tests Indicates low levels of the copper-carrying protein due to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"defective copper transport in Wilson's disease. Free Serum Copper: Increased (toxic unbound copper), reflects elevated free copper levels as it is not effectively bound to ceruloplasmin. Note: This test is not definitive for Wilson's disease because elevated free copper can occur in other conditions as well, such as liver disease or acute inflammation. 24-Hour Urinary Copper Excretion: Elevated (>100 µg/24h), A reliable indicator of copper overload, often used for diagnosis and monitoring treatment. (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Liver Biopsy Copper Content in Liver Tissue: Elevated (>250 µg/g dry"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"weight), Definitive test for Wilson's disease, measuring accumulated copper in hepatocytes. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Genetic Testin Mutation analysis of ATP7B gene: Confirms the g diagnosis; Useful in uncertain cases or for family"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"screening."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Imaging Slit-Lamp Examination: Detects Kayser-Fleischer rings,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Indicates copper deposition in the cornea. MRI Brain: Shows copper accumulation in basal ganglia, often with the \"face of the giant panda\" sign - Typical finding associated with neurological manifestations due to copper deposition. (Option B ruled out)"}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 22-year-old patient presents with neurological symptoms such as tremors, dysarthria, and gait disturbances. An MRI of the brain is performed and reveals hyperintensities at the sites given below. Based on these findings and the clinical presentation, what is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Huntington's disease"},{"t":"Multiple sclerosis"},{"t":"Wilson’s disease"},{"t":"Parkinson’s disease"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: C) Wilson’s disease"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Imaging finding in Wilson’s disease, particularly on MRI, is the \"giant panda sign\" ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is characterized by hyperintensities in the midbrain, specifically in the tectum, tegmentum, and red nucleus, with sparing of the substantia nigra, creating a pattern resembling a panda’s face on axial T2-weighted MRI."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MRI Findings and Associated Diseases"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Disease MRI Sign/Feature Radiograph"},{"k":"img","v":9524},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Wilson’s Disease Face of the Giant"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Panda Sign"},{"k":"img","v":9529},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Multiple Sclerosis Dawson’s Fingers (MS)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":9534},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Huntington’s Disease Boxcar Ventricles"},{"k":"img","v":9540},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Parkinson’s Disease Swallow Tail Sign"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":9542},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob Cortical Ribboning Disease (CJD)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":9547},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Progressive Mickey Mouse Supranuclear Palsy Sign (PSP)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Multiple System Hot Cross Bun bun sign\" data-author=\"Assoc Prof Atrophy Sign Frank Gaillard\" data-hash=\"9301\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"data-license=\"CC BY SA 4.0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikim edia.org/wiki/File:Hot_cross_bun_sig n.jpg\" data-tags=\"April2025\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"otes/cS2GEfTlNb5KPu3bNOKk1744 365672.png\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Alzheimer’s Disease Medial Temporal temporal atrophy\" data-author=\"Hurf"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Atrophy ord, Robert & Charidimou, Andreas"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"& Werring, David & Fox, Zoe & Cipolotti, Lisa & Jager, R\" data-hash=\"9302\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"https://www.researchg ate.net/figure/The-medial-temporal-a trophy-MTA-visual-rating-scale-a-d- Coronal-FLAIR-images-showing_fig2 _305615685\" data-tags=\"April2025\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"otes/XcMhho9RXKK7e7Z84s0x1744 365717.png\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Wernicke’s Encephal Mammillary Body encephalopathy mammillary body opathy Hyperintensities hyperintensities\" data-author=\"Sama"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"r A. Abbas, Halim Abboud, Moussa A. Chalah,Chadi Sabbagh, Samar S. Ayache\" data-hash=\"9304\" data-license=\"CC BY\" data-source=\"https://www.mdpi.com/ 2076-328X/8/11/104\" data-tags=\"April2025\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"otes/GITLequcBgNXxoTUIgqU1744 365755.png\" />"},{"k":"img","v":9553},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neurocysticercosis Starry Sky"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Appearance"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":9559},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Subacute Combined Inverted V Sign Degeneration"},{"k":"img","v":9564},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Glioblastoma Butterfly Glioma Multiforme (GBM)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tuberous Sclerosis Cortical Tubers Sclerosis Cortical tubers\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"data-author=\"Hellerhoff\" data-hash=\"9314\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 3.0\" data-source=\"https://com mons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tubero ese_Sklerose_1J_T2_axial2.png\" data-tags=\"April2025\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"otes/D2DtmzhG4BrKLZQzc6Xa1744 366961.png\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Central Pontine Trident Sign Pontine Myelinolysis Trident sign\" Myelinolysis (CPM) data-author=\"Jto410\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"data-hash=\"9315\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 3.0\" data-source=\"https://com mons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MRI_C entral_Pontine_Myelinolysis_fat_sat _T2.jpg\" data-tags=\"April2025\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"otes/q0hBMyaKNgWi6qBFH5Zn174 4367003.png\" />"},{"k":"img","v":9570},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Meningioma Dural Tail Sign"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"}],"qi":[9468]},{"n":7,"q":"A 24-year-old patient presents with the following symptoms: tremors, dysarthria, irritability, and impulsive behavior, along with jaundice and the presence of Kayser-Fleischer rings on slit-lamp examination. What is the best treatment approach for this condition?","o":[{"t":"Penicillamine"},{"t":"Trientine"},{"t":"Zinc acetate"},{"t":"Dimercaprol"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Penicillamine"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Penicillamine is a chelating agent that effectively binds copper, facilitating its excretion through the kidneys and reducing copper accumulation in the body."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is particularly indicated for symptomatic patients with neurological or hepatic manifestations."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of Wilson’s Disease"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chelation Therapy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Penicillamine: The first-line chelating agent that binds copper and promotes its excretion in urine. Dosage is usually started at low levels to avoid worsening of neurological symptoms. Given along with pyridoxine or vitamin B6 (counter deficiency)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Trientine (Option B): Another chelating agent used for patients intolerant to penicillamine. It is less likely to cause side effects and is as effective."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Zinc Acetate (Option C): Inhibits copper absorption from the gastrointestinal tract and is often used for maintenance therapy after initial chelation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dietary Copper Restriction:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Avoid foods high in copper, such as shellfish, liver, nuts, chocolate, and mushrooms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic and Supportive Therapy:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Liver Transplantation: Indicated in cases of fulminant liver failure or decompensated cirrhosis. Transplantation corrects the underlying metabolic defect as the new liver has a functional ATP7B gene."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic treatment of neurological features: Physical therapy for movement disorders and psychiatric care for behavioral changes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Note:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dimercaprol (Option D): Although a chelating agent, it is primarily used for the treatment of heavy metal poisoning (e.g., lead, mercury)"}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 28-year-old patient who is a known case of Wilson’s disease presents to the emergency department with acute liver failure. He has neurological symptoms and liver dysfunction. Upon evaluation, laboratory results reveal the following: Serum bilirubin: 12 mg/dL. Serum aspartate transferase (AST): 250 IU/L. Prothrombin time prolongation: 15 seconds. Based on these results, calculate the NAZER prognostic score for this patient and the next step in treatment accordingly.","o":[{"t":"NAZER score of 5; no need for transplantation"},{"t":"NAZER score of 8; medical management is sufficient"},{"t":"NAZER score of 9; liver transplantation is indicated"},{"t":"NAZER score of 11; monitor the patient without intervention"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) NAZER score of 9; liver transplantation is indicated"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The NAZER index in the above scenario is calculated as follows:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Serum bilirubin: 12 mg/dL = score 3"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Serum aspartate transferase (AST): 250 IU/L = score 3"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prothrombin time prolongation: 15 seconds = score 3"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Total score = 9."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A score of 9 or more indicates the need for liver transplantation in this patient."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Liver transplantation in Wilson’s disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Liver transplantation is an effective treatment for patients with Wilson disease who have end-stage liver disease or acute liver failure, showing excellent postoperative survival rates, especially in younger patients without recurrence."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A tailored approach for evaluating liver transplant candidates with Wilson disease and managing their care during the waiting period is proposed, emphasising the complexities of this systemic condition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Different scores and systems are in place to evaluate a candidate for liver transplantation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"NAZER prognostic index:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This index is used to calculate the prognosis of the liver disease and determine the need for liver transplantation. Laboratory Score (in measurement points)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Normal value 0 1 2 3 4"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Serum bilirubi 0.2-1.2 <5.8 5.8-8.8 8.8-11. 11.7-17. >17. n mg/dL 7 5 5"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Serum aspart 10-35 IU/L <100 100-15 151-20 201-300 >300 ate transferas 0 0 e"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prolongation <4 4-8 9-12 13-20 >20 of prothrombi n time (secon ds)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A failed medical treatment indicates the need for liver transplantation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is indicated in a patient with acute liver failure due to Wilson disease with a NAZER prognostic score of 9 or more."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Child-Pugh score:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The prognosis of chronic liver disease can be assessed using the Child-Pugh score."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It uses five clinical measures, each measure is given scores from 1-3 according to the grades, and interpreted as follows: Factor Points toward total score"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1 2 3"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"S. bilirubin (mg/dL) <2 2-3 >3"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"S. albumin (g/dL) >3.5 3-3.5 <3"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prothrombin time <4 4-6 >6 (seconds) Or <1.7 1.7-2.3 >2.3 INR"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ascites None Easily controlled Poorly controlled"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hepatic encephalopathy None Minimal Advanced"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Category Points Description"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Class A 5 to 6 Well-preserved liver function"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Class B 7 to 9 Moderate liver function"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Class C 10 to 15 Severe liver dysfunction with a life"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"expectancy of approximately one year"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transplantation is indicated in patients with a score >/= 7, i.e., of categories B and C."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Model for End-stage Liver Disease/MELD:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Highly effective predictor of 3-month mortality for cirrhotic patients awaiting orthotopic liver transplantation, considering three factors as, Serum creatine Serum bilirubin International normalised ratio (INR) of prothrombin time"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum creatine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum bilirubin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"International normalised ratio (INR) of prothrombin time"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MELD score ‡20: Higher mortality rate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum creatine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum bilirubin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"International normalised ratio (INR) of prothrombin time"}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 38-year-old woman presents with symptoms of fatigue, arthralgia, and new-onset diabetes. Her family history reveals that her father was diagnosed with hemochromatosis. Further evaluations show significantly elevated serum ferritin and transferrin saturation levels and that she carries both the C282Y and H63D mutations in the HFE gene. Based on her clinical presentation and genetic findings, which type of hemochromatosis is she most likely experiencing?","o":[{"t":"Type 1"},{"t":"Type 2"},{"t":"Type 3"},{"t":"Type 4"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Type 1"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical scenario points to the diagnosis of Type 1 hereditary hemochromatosis as the patient has both C282/H63D mutations along with a family history ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hemochromatosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemochromatosis is a common inherited disorder of iron metabolism, particularly prevalent in European populations."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is characterised by genetic variability but ultimately leads to excessive iron release into cells, resulting in increased intestinal iron absorption and tissue damage."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definition :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hemochromatosis refers to a group of genetic disorders that predispose individuals to iron overload, potentially causing organ damage and failure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Types of hemochromatosis :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hereditary hemochromatosis Secondary"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type 1 HFE-related Iron-loading anemias (Option A) C282Y homozygosity Thalassemia major"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"C282/H63D compound Siderroblastioc anemia heterozygosity Chronic hemolytic anemias"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Transfusion and parenteral iron overload Dietary iron overload"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type-2 Non-HFE related Chronic liver disease (Option B) Juvenile hemochromatosis Hepatitis C"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Alcoholic cirrhosis Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis Porphyria cutanea tarda Dysmerabolic iron overload syndrome Post-porta caval shunting"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type-3 Mutated transferrin receptor 2 (Option C) (TRF2)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type-4 Mutated ferroportin 1 gene (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hereditary hemochromatosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron metabolism disruption: Normal body iron content is approximately 3–4 g. In hemochromatosis, intestinal iron absorption exceeds body needs, leading to ‡4 mg/d absorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal body iron content is approximately 3–4 g."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In hemochromatosis, intestinal iron absorption exceeds body needs, leading to ‡4 mg/d absorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron accumulation: The excess iron accumulates in plasma, increasing transferrin saturation and plasma ferritin levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The excess iron accumulates in plasma, increasing transferrin saturation and plasma ferritin levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Role of hepcidin (HEP atic bacterio CID al prote IN) : Hepcidin, a liver-derived peptide, regulates iron absorption and release by binding to ferroportin. HFE, TFR2, and hemojuvelin influence hepcidin production, and any mutations lead to reduced hepcidin and increased iron absorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepcidin, a liver-derived peptide, regulates iron absorption and release by binding to ferroportin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HFE, TFR2, and hemojuvelin influence hepcidin production, and any mutations lead to reduced hepcidin and increased iron absorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic basis: A common mutation in the HFE gene (C282Y) impairs cell surface expression and reduces hepcidin production, leading to increased dietary iron absorption."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Advanced disease can result in iron levels exceeding 20 g, deposited primarily in the liver,pancreas, and heart."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A common mutation in the HFE gene (C282Y) impairs cell surface expression and reduces hepcidin production, leading to increased dietary iron absorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advanced disease can result in iron levels exceeding 20 g, deposited primarily in the liver, pancreas, and heart."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal body iron content is approximately 3–4 g."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In hemochromatosis, intestinal iron absorption exceeds body needs, leading to ‡4 mg/d absorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The excess iron accumulates in plasma, increasing transferrin saturation and plasma ferritin levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepcidin, a liver-derived peptide, regulates iron absorption and release by binding to ferroportin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HFE, TFR2, and hemojuvelin influence hepcidin production, and any mutations lead to reduced hepcidin and increased iron absorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A common mutation in the HFE gene (C282Y) impairs cell surface expression and reduces hepcidin production, leading to increased dietary iron absorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advanced disease can result in iron levels exceeding 20 g, deposited primarily in the liver, pancreas, and heart."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Secondary iron overload:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conditions like sideroblastic anemia and thalassemia lead to increased iron absorption and can result in similar complications as hemochromatosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron overload in porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) may exacerbate existing enzyme deficiencies."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Environmental Factors:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Excessive iron ingestion over the years is a rare cause, but notable cases include individuals consuming iron from brewing vessels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Long-term medicinal iron use can lead to hemochromatosis in genetically predisposed individuals."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 45-year-old male presents to the clinic with complaints of fatigue, joint pain, and a noticeable change in skin color, described as a slate-gray hue. He reports a family history of liver disease and diabetes. Blood tests reveal elevated serum ferritin and transferrin saturation levels. Which of the following clinical features, once developed, progresses even after successful treatment of the condition?","o":[{"t":"Hepatomegaly"},{"t":"Cardiac arrhythmia"},{"t":"Arthropathy"},{"t":"Hypogonadism"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Arthropathy"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The scenario here is of hemochromatosis where excess iron is seen in the blood and as deposits in the tissues and also in joints, leading to arthropathy, which is progressive and continues to progress even after normalization of the iron levels by treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of Hemochromatosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The stages of progression of the disease are as follows: Genetic predisposition without abnormalities. Iron overload without symptoms. Iron overload with symptoms (e.g., arthritis, fatigue). Iron overload with organ damage, particularly cirrhosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic predisposition without abnormalities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron overload without symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron overload with symptoms (e.g., arthritis, fatigue)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron overload with organ damage, particularly cirrhosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In asymptomatic cases: Significant iron overload can occur without any symptoms. Up to 56% of asymptomatic C282Y homozygotes show hepatic iron overload."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Significant iron overload can occur without any symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Up to 56% of asymptomatic C282Y homozygotes show hepatic iron overload."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initial symptoms may be nonspecific like: Lethargy. Arthralgia. Skin pigmentation. Loss of libido. Signs of diabetes mellitus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lethargy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arthralgia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Skin pigmentation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of libido."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signs of diabetes mellitus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advanced Symptoms: The liver is the first organ affected. Hepatomegaly is seen in >95% of symptomatic patients. (Option A) Excessive skin pigmentation, characterized by a metallic or slate-gray hue called bronzing is seen due to melanin and iron deposits in the dermis. Spider angiomas, splenomegaly, ascites. Cardiac issues , including arrhythmias, Dilated cardiomyopathy and congestive heart failure. Loss of body hair and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The liver is the first organ affected."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatomegaly is seen in >95% of symptomatic patients. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Excessive skin pigmentation, characterized by a metallic or slate-gray hue called bronzing is seen due to melanin and iron deposits in the dermis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spider angiomas, splenomegaly, ascites."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiac issues , including arrhythmias, Dilated cardiomyopathy and congestive heart failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of body hair and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic predisposition without abnormalities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron overload without symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron overload with symptoms (e.g., arthritis, fatigue)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron overload with organ damage, particularly cirrhosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Significant iron overload can occur without any symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Up to 56% of asymptomatic C282Y homozygotes show hepatic iron overload."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lethargy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arthralgia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Skin pigmentation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of libido."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signs of diabetes mellitus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The liver is the first organ affected."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatomegaly is seen in >95% of symptomatic patients. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Excessive skin pigmentation, characterized by a metallic or slate-gray hue called bronzing is seen due to melanin and iron deposits in the dermis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spider angiomas, splenomegaly, ascites."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiac issues , including arrhythmias, Dilated cardiomyopathy and congestive heart failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of body hair and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism."},{"k":"img","v":9583},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetes Mellitus: Occurs in approximately 65% of patients with advanced disease, often linked to pancreatic damage from iron deposition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occurs in approximately 65% of patients with advanced disease, often linked to pancreatic damage from iron deposition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arthropathy : Small joint, non-erosive, non-inflammatory arthropathy. The 2nd and 3rd metacarpophalangeal joints are the most commonly affected first. Progressive polyarthritis and acute synovitis may occur. Radiologic findings include cystic changes, cartilage loss,and bone proliferation. This arthropathy is progressive and does not alleviate with treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small joint, non-erosive, non-inflammatory arthropathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The 2nd and 3rd metacarpophalangeal joints are the most commonly affected first."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive polyarthritis and acute synovitis may occur."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiologic findings include cystic changes, cartilage loss, and bone proliferation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This arthropathy is progressive and does not alleviate with treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occurs in approximately 65% of patients with advanced disease, often linked to pancreatic damage from iron deposition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small joint, non-erosive, non-inflammatory arthropathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The 2nd and 3rd metacarpophalangeal joints are the most commonly affected first."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive polyarthritis and acute synovitis may occur."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiologic findings include cystic changes, cartilage loss, and bone proliferation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This arthropathy is progressive and does not alleviate with treatment."},{"k":"img","v":9589},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiac involvement: Congestive heart failure Cardiac arrhythmias like: (Option B) Premature supraventricular beats Paroxysmal tachyarrhythmias Atrial flutter Atrial fibrillation Varying degrees of atrioventricular block"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Congestive heart failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiac arrhythmias like: (Option B) Premature supraventricular beats Paroxysmal tachyarrhythmias Atrial flutter Atrial fibrillation Varying degrees of atrioventricular block"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Premature supraventricular beats"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paroxysmal tachyarrhythmias"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial flutter"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Varying degrees of atrioventricular block"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pituitary involvement (hypogonadism) : (Option D) Reduced gonadotropin production as a result of impaired hypothalamic-pituitary function by iron deposits (in both sexes). Manifestations include: Loss of libido Impotence Amenorrhea Testicular atrophy Gynecomastia Sparse body hair"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduced gonadotropin production as a result of impaired hypothalamic-pituitary function by iron deposits (in both sexes)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Manifestations include: Loss of libido Impotence Amenorrhea Testicular atrophy Gynecomastia Sparse body hair"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of libido"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impotence"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amenorrhea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Testicular atrophy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gynecomastia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sparse body hair"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Congestive heart failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiac arrhythmias like: (Option B) Premature supraventricular beats Paroxysmal tachyarrhythmias Atrial flutter Atrial fibrillation Varying degrees of atrioventricular block"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Premature supraventricular beats"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paroxysmal tachyarrhythmias"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial flutter"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Varying degrees of atrioventricular block"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Premature supraventricular beats"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paroxysmal tachyarrhythmias"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial flutter"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Varying degrees of atrioventricular block"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduced gonadotropin production as a result of impaired hypothalamic-pituitary function by iron deposits (in both sexes)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Manifestations include: Loss of libido Impotence Amenorrhea Testicular atrophy Gynecomastia Sparse body hair"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of libido"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impotence"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amenorrhea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Testicular atrophy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gynecomastia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sparse body hair"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of libido"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impotence"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amenorrhea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Testicular atrophy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gynecomastia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sparse body hair"}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with fatigue, joint pain, and new-onset diabetes. He has a family history of liver disease and reports high alcohol intake. Which biochemical test would be a strong indicator of hemochromatosis in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Plasma iron levels"},{"t":"Serum ferritin levels"},{"t":"Fasting serum transferrin saturation"},{"t":"Liver biopsy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Serum ferritin levels"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The best indicator out of all the biochemical tests for hemochromatosis would be the serum ferritin levels, as this is also an indicator of the body's iron stores ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical indicators:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Key symptoms suggesting hemochromatosis include: Hepatomegaly Skin pigmentation changes Diabetes mellitus Heart disease Arthritis Hypogonadism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Skin pigmentation changes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetes mellitus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heart disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arthritis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypogonadism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Significant iron overload may occur without all symptoms present, necessitating a high level of suspicion for early diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Skin pigmentation changes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetes mellitus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heart disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arthritis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypogonadism"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Patient history:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Detailed family history of similar diseases is crucial."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inquire about alcohol consumption, iron intake , and high doses of vitamin C, which can increase iron absorption."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Physical Examination:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Confirm signs of liver, pancreatic, cardiac, and joint disease through examination and relevant imaging tests."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Biochemical Testing:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Methods to evaluate total body iron include:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigation Interpretation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Plasma iron level Elevated (not reliable) (vs Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Serum transferrin Elevated: Iron overload (may have false positives) (vs saturation (fasting) Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":">45%: Risk for hemochromatosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Serum ferritin level >1000 mg/L: Disease in C282Y homozygotes (a reliable (Option B) indicator of body iron stores and is significantly elevated"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"in hemochromatosis)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Role of liver biopsy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It measures liver iron concentration and calculates the hepatic iron index."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It provides definitive information on liver fibrosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Predict the prognosis and risk of forming hepatocellular carcinoma."},{"k":"img","v":9597},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CT/MRI:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Help determine the iron concentration in the liver by the increased density in the images."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MRI is more accurate for this."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"They are non-invasive and, hence, are sometimes preferred over biopsy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Liver biopsy (Option D) is a very important method for diagnosing the level of damage to the organ and measures the liver iron concentration in a patient, helping in detecting fibrosis and cirrhosis, but it is not a biochemical test as asked in this scenario. It is used in cases of non-HFE hemochromatosis and those with dual pathology."}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 25-year-old female comes with fatigue, joint pains, and a slate-gray appearance of the skin. After evaluation and detailed history, the physician suspects hemochromatosis. Which of the following statements about screening for hemochromatosis is false?","o":[{"t":"All adult first-degree relatives should be tested for C282Y and H63D mutations."},{"t":"Genetic testing for the C282Y and H63D mutations is recommended if screening tests are abnormal."},{"t":"Children of diagnosed individuals under 18 years old should always be screened for hemochromatosis."},{"t":"Screening is done using transferrin saturation and ferritin levels."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Children of diagnosed individuals under 18 years old should always be screened for hemochromatosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In the case of a parent with hemochromatosis, the testing of children is generally not necessary until they are 18 or if the other parent’s HFE mutation is confirmed."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Screening for hemochromatosis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This is done once the diagnosis of hemochromatosis is made."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Family screening: Involves counseling and testing the family members of diagnosed individuals, as asymptomatic relatives often show increased transferrin saturation and ferritin levels. All adult first-degree relatives should be tested for C282Y and H63D mutations. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involves counseling and testing the family members of diagnosed individuals, as asymptomatic relatives often show increased transferrin saturation and ferritin levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"All adult first-degree relatives should be tested for C282Y and H63D mutations. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Testing in children: Testing of children is generally not necessary until they are 18 unless the other parent’s HFE mutation status is known."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Testing of children is generally not necessary until they are 18 unless the other parent’s HFE mutation status is known."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Controversy in population screening: Screening using transferrin saturation and ferritin levels is effective for detecting hemochromatosis and iron deficiency. (Option D) Genetic screening in the general population is feasible, but its cost-effectiveness is debated."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Screening using transferrin saturation and ferritin levels is effective for detecting hemochromatosis and iron deficiency. (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic screening in the general population is feasible, but its cost-effectiveness is debated."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involves counseling and testing the family members of diagnosed individuals, as asymptomatic relatives often show increased transferrin saturation and ferritin levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"All adult first-degree relatives should be tested for C282Y and H63D mutations. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Testing of children is generally not necessary until they are 18 unless the other parent’s HFE mutation status is known."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Screening using transferrin saturation and ferritin levels is effective for detecting hemochromatosis and iron deficiency. (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic screening in the general population is feasible, but its cost-effectiveness is debated."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Genetic testing:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic testing for the C282Y and H63D mutations is recommended if screening tests are abnormal. (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hereditary hemochromatosis is a genetically diverse condition influenced by both genetic and acquired factors."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The detection of the HFE gene helps in finding the genetic component of the disease."}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 45-year-old male patient with a confirmed diagnosis of hereditary hemochromatosis presents with symptoms of fatigue, diabetes, and joint pain. Laboratory tests reveal elevated serum ferritin levels of 2000 mg/dl. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Deferoxamine parenteral chelation therapy"},{"t":"Liver transplantation"},{"t":"Deferiprone oral chelation therapy"},{"t":"Phlebotomy"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Phlebotomy"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The first and foremost treatment modality for effective removal of excessive iron from the blood is phlebotomy, which can effectively remove 200-250 mg of iron from 500 ml of blood in one sitting of the procedure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of hemochromatosis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The treatment of hemochromatosis aims towards the removal of excess iron and supportive management of the affected organs and is explained below:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1) Iron removal:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Phlebotomy (most preferred treatment) :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The primary method for removing excess iron involves weekly or biweekly phlebotomy of 500 mL, which contains 200-250 mg of iron ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plasma ferritin concentration decreases ( reduced iron stores) ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plasma transferrin saturation remains elevated."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For advanced disease: Weekly phlebotomy until serum ferritin levels reach £100 mg/L."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ferritin levels after treatment £100 mg/L."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chelation therapy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indication: Phlebotomy is unfeasible (anemia or hypoproteinemia)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Phlebotomy is unfeasible (anemia or hypoproteinemia)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Drugs: Deferoxamine (Option A) Parenteral chelating agent (removes 10-20 mg of iron per) Deferasirox (Exjade) and Deferiprone: (Option C) Oral chelating agents are indicated for thalassemia and secondary iron overload."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deferoxamine (Option A) Parenteral chelating agent (removes 10-20 mg of iron per)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parenteral chelating agent (removes 10-20 mg of iron per)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deferasirox (Exjade) and Deferiprone: (Option C) Oral chelating agents are indicated for thalassemia and secondary iron overload."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral chelating agents are indicated for thalassemia and secondary iron overload."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Phlebotomy is unfeasible (anemia or hypoproteinemia)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deferoxamine (Option A) Parenteral chelating agent (removes 10-20 mg of iron per)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parenteral chelating agent (removes 10-20 mg of iron per)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deferasirox (Exjade) and Deferiprone: (Option C) Oral chelating agents are indicated for thalassemia and secondary iron overload."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral chelating agents are indicated for thalassemia and secondary iron overload."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parenteral chelating agent (removes 10-20 mg of iron per)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral chelating agents are indicated for thalassemia and secondary iron overload."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2) Lifestyle adjustments:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcohol consumption: it should be minimized or eliminated, as it significantly increases cirrhosis risk in hereditary hemochromatosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dietary changes: Generally unnecessary, but avoid vitamin C and iron supplements."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3) Management of complications:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatic failure, cardiac failure, and diabetes mellitus are treated similarly to conventional approaches for these conditions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of libido and changes in secondary sex characteristics can be addressed with testosterone replacement or gonadotropin therapy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4) Liver transplantation: (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This may be indicated in end-stage liver disease, but better outcomes can be seen if excess iron is removed before the procedure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Successful liver transplantation can reverse the underlying metabolic issues related to hemochromatosis."}]}]);
